{
  "summary": "Expands shortened IPv6 addresses containing \"::\" into full eight-group notation before sending them as domain names in SOCKS5 CONNECT requests. This prevents Tor exit relays from misparsing destination \"<address>:<port>\" strings as an IPv6 address without a port when the port is absorbed as an eighth group. Zone indices and addresses already using all eight groups are left unchanged.",
  "problem": "Outbound connections through SOCKS5 proxies using Tor fail with \"general failure\" whenever an IPv6 peer's address contains an inner \"::\". Tor exits attempt to parse \"<name>:<port>\" as an address, causing the compressed address to absorb the port number and fail port extraction, dropping roughly 20% of IPv6 peers in addrman and 29% of DNS seed IPv6 answers with no fallback.",
  "evidence": [
    "Corroborates with upstream Tor issue #32314 where Tor exit relays misparse compressed IPv6 destinations with appended ports.",
    "Author reports: \"About 20% of the IPv6 addresses in a gossip-filled addrman, and 29% of current IPv6 DNS seed answers, have an inner '::'. The failure is closed; there is no fallback to a direct connection.\"",
    "Mainnet testing against Tor 0.4.9.13: \"master could not reach four seed-provided peers with inner-'::' addresses (30 'general failure'); with this change all four connected and completed the version handshake.\""
  ],
